en't blown down by the Category 2 winds; they were physically crushed and washed away by the water. **3. The scale actually *used* to include water, but it failed.** Originally, the Saffir-Simpson scale *did* try to include storm surge. A Category 3 was supposed to have a 9- to 12-foot surge, a Category 4 a 13- to 18-foot surge, etc. But in 2010, the National Hurricane Center stripped water out of the scale entirely. Why? Because storms like Ike (a Cat 2 with a Cat 5 surge) and Charley (a Cat 4 with a Cat 2 surge) proved that wind speed and water height don't always match up. They made the Saffir-Simpson scale a **wind-only scale** to keep it scientifically accurate to what Saffir designed. **The Bottom Line:** You are exactly right that the Saffir-Simpson scale is just a wind-damage measurement. But that is exactly why meteorologists have to talk about water, size, and speed. If you only look at the wind scale, you are only measuring *half* the storm's weapons. If a doctor only measures your heart rate but ignores your blood pressure, they aren't getting the full picture of your health. The same is true for hurricanes: wind tells us what will happen to the roofs, but water tells us what will happen to the foundations.
